The exact matching `@riducms/cli` is a project-local development dependency. Ridu starts the
development database, keeps generated
contracts current, runs the Go API and Vite admin together, and prints the local URLs. Edit
`content/*.go` to change your CMS. Schema changes reload the open admin after the new Go server is
ready; Svelte component changes use Vite hot module replacement.{{if .MongoDB}} By default, development uses the
pinned single-node MongoDB replica set in `compose.yaml`. Pass `--database-url` or set
`DATABASE_URL` to use another replica set; configuration discovery and `{{.RiduCommand}} generate` never open
that database.{{end}}

If you enable the optional GraphQL plugin, configure `generated.graphql.schema` in `ridu.toml` so ordinary
generation also commits and drift-checks its exact SDL for client tooling.

On an empty database, open the admin and create the first `users` account in the guided setup
screen. Ridu serializes that anonymous first-user operation with the credential write, so exactly
one contender can succeed. Afterward, the generated collection policies require authentication;
anonymous registration is not enabled accidentally.{{if .Starter}} The starter includes Posts as a small,
deletable example.{{end}} See `PROJECT.md` for the non-interactive REST shape and for how to opt into
intentional public registration.

{{if .MongoDB}}MongoDB production support in this release is deliberately bounded to ordinary generated starter
and blank projects on Linux x86-64, using digest-pinned MongoDB Community 8.2.9 as a writable three-member
replica set with SCRAM-SHA-256 authentication and CA- and hostname-verified TLS. Give
the server a database-scoped application credential. The release gate proves that application
identity is separate from one controlled operational identity used for both shadow verification
and backup/restore. Deployment operators should split those duties into separately scoped
verification and backup credentials where their MongoDB role model permits; that further split is
an operator responsibility, not release-gate evidence. Never supply operational credentials to the
application process. Local ARM runs are preflight only; the production support evidence is the
GitHub Linux x86-64 release job.

For every release with a new migration artifact, create and review that immutable artifact—even
when a data-only transform leaves the manifest unchanged. Confirm each detected rename during
`{{.RiduCommand}} migrate create` (or use reviewed `--accept-renames` automation), and bind a registered
compiled transform with `--transform` when stored data needs an explicit rewrite. When
planning reports reviewed transform or resource-retirement work as destructive, rerun the same
create command with `--allow-destructive`; that flag publishes the reviewed intent but does not
open or change the database. Checksum-authenticated planner `1.0.0` artifacts remain the supported
v1-to-v2 immutable history prefix; planner `2.0.0` owns new semantic rename, transform, and
retirement steps. Never rewrite the v1 prefix during an upgrade. `{{.RiduCommand}} check` and `{{.RiduCommand}} build` remain offline: they compare
the exact committed history with the executable, but do not inspect the applied database ledger or
indexes. Live `status` and startup/readiness own those checks.

Cut over in this order:

1. **Drain writers.** Stop every application process, worker, and other writer, and keep them
   drained through completion and retries.
2. **Verify history.** Run `{{.RiduCommand}} migrate verify` with the operator's scoped verification
   credential. Append `--allow-maintenance` whenever the complete committed history contains
   semantic work, because the clean shadow replays every artifact. Grant it only after the drain.
3. **Capture the recovery point.** Use the operator's controlled backup credential for a
   database-scoped `mongodump` and copy the matching upload objects while writers remain drained.
   Restore that scope with `mongorestore` and the matching uploads if recovery is required.
4. **Apply migrations.** Run `{{.RiduCommand}} migrate up`. Append `--allow-maintenance` only when the pending
   or incomplete history suffix contains semantic work.
5. **Confirm status and readiness.** Require `{{.RiduCommand}} migrate status` to report the exact history
   current and retain the non-mutating exact-readiness gate for that ordered artifact fingerprint,
   manifest digest, and its required indexes.
6. **Start the release.** Start the new binary; it must pass exact readiness before binding its
   listener and serving traffic.

This qualification does not cover Atlas, Amazon DocumentDB, Azure Cosmos DB, standalone servers,
other MongoDB versions or topologies, other operating systems or architectures, arbitrary scale,
network-partition matrices, or point-in-time recovery.

Before release:

```sh
{{.RiduCommand}} migrate create --name schema-change
{{.RiduCommand}} generate --check
{{.RiduCommand}} check
{{.RiduCommand}} build
# Append --allow-maintenance whenever the complete history contains semantic work.
DATABASE_URL="$MONGODB_VERIFY_URL" {{.RiduCommand}} migrate verify
# Capture a database-scoped mongodump and the matching upload objects here.
# Append --allow-maintenance only when the pending or incomplete suffix contains semantic work.
DATABASE_URL="$MONGODB_APPLICATION_URL" {{.RiduCommand}} migrate up
DATABASE_URL="$MONGODB_APPLICATION_URL" {{.RiduCommand}} migrate status
DATABASE_URL="$MONGODB_APPLICATION_URL" ./dist/{{.ProjectName}}
```
